# What must never reach a Vercel build.
#
# `vercel deploy` from the CLI uploads the working directory. A git-triggered
# deploy only ever sees committed files, so this file is what protects the
# CLI path — and the difference is not theoretical: a CLI deploy shipped
# prisma/dev.db (2.4 MB, the development database with real fixtures in it),
# and `prisma migrate deploy` then ran against that file instead of the real
# database. It has tables but no migration history, so the build failed with
# P3005 and the deployment errored.
#
# Local databases. Uploading one is both a build hazard and a data leak: the
# development database holds real client rows.
prisma/dev.db
prisma/test.db
*.db
*.db-journal
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# Local secrets. Vercel injects project environment variables at build time
# and never reads an uploaded .env, so shipping one gains nothing and puts
# credentials in a build context.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Build and tooling output — regenerated by the build, and large.
.next
node_modules
coverage
playwright-report
test-results

# Agent and editor scratch space, including git worktrees.
.claude
.vercel
